  "result": "I wrote the network by hand and wrote the notes. One `forward.py` check on 20 points (1 of the 5 allowed calls) gave every logit the right sign.\n\n**The weights (`written.json`):**\n- **Hidden neurons:** h1 = relu(x+y), h2 = relu(\u2212x\u2212y), h3 = relu(x\u2212y), h4 = relu(\u2212x+y). All biases are 0.\n- **Output:** weights [1, 1, \u22121, \u22121] with bias 0, so logit = |x+y| \u2212 |x\u2212y|.\n\n**Why it's correct:** |a| \u2212 |b| always has the same sign as a\u00b2 \u2212 b\u00b2, and (x+y)\u00b2 \u2212 (x\u2212y)\u00b2 = 4xy. So the logit's sign equals the sign of x\u00b7y for every point, not just inside [\u22121,1]\u00b2. Written out, logit = 2\u00b7sign(xy)\u00b7min(|x|,|y|).\n\nThat makes the decision boundary exactly the two axes, with no approximation. Points on the axes give a logit of exactly 0, which the spec allows since x\u00b7y = 0 there.\n\n**The check:** I tested the corners, quadrant centres, points very close to the axes like (\u00b10.01, 0.9) and (0.001, \u22121), and points on the axes. The near-axis points came out at \u00b10.02 and \u00b10.002, and the on-axis points at exactly 0.\n\nThe explanation is in `notes.md`, under 20 lines.",
